  "response": "I have successfully implemented the 1-bit rendering system for the OpenClaw game on Flipper Zero.\n\n1.  **Rendering Logic (`openclaw_render.c`, `openclaw_render.h`)**:\n    -   Implemented `openclaw_render_tilemap()` to calculate the visible viewport and draw only the necessary tiles, fetching them via the asset manager.\n    -   Implemented `openclaw_render_player()` to draw the player sprite. It includes logic to select sprites based on state (Idle, Walk, Jump) using ID offsets (200, 201, 202) and falls back to a procedural drawing if assets are missing.\n    -   Implemented `openclaw_camera_update()` to smoothly follow the player while keeping the viewport within the map bounds.\n\n2.  **Asset Handling**:\n    -   Created a helper `openclaw_draw_sprite` to handle 1-byte-per-pixel sprite data (as loaded by the existing asset system) and render it efficiently to the Flipper's 1-bit canvas, including horizontal flipping support.\n\n3.  **Optimization**:\n    -   **Cache Size**: Increased `OPENCLAW_TILE_CACHE_SIZE` in `openclaw_assets.h` from 16 to 64. This prevents cache thrashing when rendering a full screen of unique tiles (which requires ~32 tiles).\n    -   **Efficient Drawing**: The rendering loop bounds checking and opaque pixel skipping ensure minimal CPU usage.\n\nThe system is now ready to be integrated into the main application loop.\n\n**Files Created/Modified:**\n-   `applications/external/openclaw/openclaw_render.h` (New)\n-   `applications/external/openclaw/openclaw_render.c` (New)\n-   `applications/external/openclaw/openclaw_assets.h` (Modified)",
